[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r11
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: FRR EIGRP process on r11 (the OSPF↔EIGRP redistribution boundary). Mechanism: Redistribution is one-way only. OSPF is currently redistributing EIGRP routes, so the OSPF side learns EIGRP prefixes, but the reverse (OSPF into EIGRP) is not occurring. This leaves the EIGRP domain without specific OSPF routes, even though forwarding still works via a default route.

Localization and evidence:
- On the OSPF side, r1 learns the EIGRP domain: r1 shows 120.0.0.0/8 via OSPF through r11 (show ip route 120.0.0.0/8 -> known via "ospf", via 111.1.1.2).
- On the EIGRP side, r12 does not learn OSPF routes: show ip route 20.0.0.0/8 on r12 -> “% Network not in table.”
- r11’s config confirms asymmetry: router ospf has “redistribute eigrp”, but router eigrp 100 has no “redistribute ospf” (nor a default-metric), so OSPF prefixes are not injected into EIGRP.
- End-to-end pings still succeed (pc0 ↔ pc12 reachability OK) because r12 has a default route via r11 (0.0.0.0/0 via 112.1.1.1), masking the missing specific OSPF routes.

Root cause: The “redistribute ospf” configuration under EIGRP on r11 was removed or not restored during maintenance, breaking OSPF-to-EIGRP redistribution.

Corrective fix: On r11, under router eigrp 100, re-enable redistribution of OSPF into EIGRP, ideally with an explicit default metric, for example:
- router eigrp 100
  redistribute ospf
  eigrp default-metric <bw> <delay> <reliability> <loading> <mtu>
Optionally apply route-maps/filters per policy. This will restore two-way redistribution and populate EIGRP with 20.x and other OSPF prefixes. === END ===